#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634699} :activity/id #uuid "f707da3e-64ca-5eeb-aee5-efb6cf24e12f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634725 :topic/id #uuid "b42483d3-3ed0-5381-808b-e4606d462d24" :topic/math-academy-id 1800} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634728 :tutorial/content "To write the equation of a line that passes through a point $P$ (with position vector $\\mathbf{p}$) and has a direction vector $\\mathbf{v},$ one option is to use the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r &=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another way, which is to write the **equation of a line using the cross product**, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-  \\mathbf{p} ) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have a line that passes through the point $P(4,1,-5)$ and has a direction vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 1,0,-1 \\rangle$ (see the picture below).\n\n![](images/6c/6cd052e948a46524e08b1305a59019b052e71c7b9ce6e5376f49c4cbcc56a737.png)\n\nThe equation of the line can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 4,1,-5 \\rangle) \\times \\langle 1,0,-1 \\rangle = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTo understand why the formula works, notice that for any point $R,$ this point lies on the line if and only if the vector $\\overrightarrow{PR}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}.$ Now, we know that the cross product of two parallel vectors is always $\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR} \\parallel \\mathbf{v} \n\t\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \n\t\\overrightarrow{PR} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, since $\\overrightarrow{PR} = \\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p},$ we get the desired equation\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "561a4258-eba4-511e-9c5b-75bf4de09801" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3247 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "052673c3-9d85-5e36-8e43-85f94f953678" :step/math-academy-id 17893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634729 :tutorial/content "If we have an equation of a line in the form $\\left( \\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p} \\right) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we can expand the parentheses, though we must remember that the cross product is non-commutative. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are both constant vectors, we can denote $\\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{p} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ to simplify the equation. So, we obtain an alternative form of the equation of the line using the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{c}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d9d8623-8c1b-54a8-8923-62467688f5e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3250 :tutorial/title "Alternative Form of the Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product"} :step/id #uuid "0cfe4750-b8f2-5f8f-b002-338e2c0d9c20" :step/math-academy-id 17900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634556 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634576 :question/id #uuid "2739d633-c98c-511d-b29b-7a186a02ea79" :question/math-academy-id "e-4916"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd88a3e9-cfa1-5b3f-b1c5-6c1939e13dd8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642349}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Canonical and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "4d9aae50-2188-5d30-b397-fa50bf19c71b" :step/math-academy-id 17895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634549 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634599 :question/id #uuid "45543759-b9e7-5e67-ac70-bc2614404f0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4918"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2393b236-cce0-5757-8e35-3d35cc7601ae" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634672}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Vector and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "b56ff46c-db1c-5ceb-a898-e82930114225" :step/math-academy-id 17897 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634557 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634647 :question/id #uuid "96216bf8-a1cf-5704-8edb-81c3fbdebb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-4922"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fecaf27b-659c-59dd-887f-f154d5f83bd4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649977}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cross Product Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "d80b6fc2-ecd5-57be-bc5e-b5525b595a71" :step/math-academy-id 17902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634552 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634640 :question/id #uuid "85e30267-bea2-5c0d-9a8b-db7f6fd16b0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "714f4f34-4ef2-528e-9229-5ac73bddc038" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642329}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Parametric and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "dba17dcb-861d-58a3-998f-944fccf2581d" :step/math-academy-id 17898 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634700}}] :activity/title "Writing the Vector Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633804}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642397} :activity/id #uuid "27418534-46a6-59f7-a2ab-b40d811f93eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634725 :topic/id #uuid "b42483d3-3ed0-5381-808b-e4606d462d24" :topic/math-academy-id 1800} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef3f09a-7011-5b20-b185-122d00dad385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641951} {:answer/id #uuid "77170d10-5fba-56c3-b03d-75d19b5b6f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642010} {:answer/id #uuid "afa6c5fe-5092-5f3e-b24c-ce9933d454c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642088} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ce2be-301f-5162-8da8-b51a17fdb34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642139} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f32b1b-134e-5861-b636-ae782fcbc7a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "69660fd4-bc3a-5b83-b17d-205a881e454d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72112" :question/problem "The parametric equations of a straight line are given by \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4-t \\\\ y &=7-7t \\\\ z &=6-6t,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The corresponding vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-t \\\\ 7-7t \\\\ 6-6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ -7t \\\\ -6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\nr=p+vt,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(4,7,6)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0146c564-bc00-5b96-8ba7-47dbf8d594f3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20344816-0c4d-5f71-883f-36ce4ce0f014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641885} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb2ed73-37a2-5040-bed2-a8bb8219af1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641900} {:answer/id #uuid "686d4b8d-c2bb-5ed8-9e72-48b6c7214447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641991} {:answer/id #uuid "70d921ac-a158-57ac-9f5a-5dfa22d3d6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641998} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc05459-8160-51bc-8233-f04b1ea5ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642072} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f5a63e-b301-573a-8ec7-a466b5120856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d9cc243-e6fc-5de1-bbe8-fb59d792510a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72073" :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x-6}{5}=\\frac{y-7}{4}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe also have the position vector $p=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ for the point $P(6,7,-3)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01adeb6e-aab9-52ad-a76c-9b34ba4f3078" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c061e4-67ee-5e5a-be1e-514c4cda73d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641890} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0dfd02-aaa2-5a75-b840-93b8961a4901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642023} {:answer/id #uuid "99bab901-099f-5a5e-a5a6-a53cb2ce4c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642068} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc9c34d-c9e8-5152-a5c5-a9e17efadd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642105} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8d226-0756-59e4-b8ba-e4610ef6696b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642151} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f529e7-19be-5b76-b53d-ba19875278ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a32b940-c8d6-532a-addf-5cc39f045684" :question/math-academy-id "q-32865" :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.$\nFind a vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe also have the position vector $p=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ for the point $P(0,-1,3)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9001be9d-f24a-51e6-9173-eaa6bd7ec5f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4e5162-7c7b-546f-ad92-ac22806bc1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=20i-40j+20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641840} {:answer/id #uuid "1db4e40f-f81a-5f5a-b6b6-e334f1dc8d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=-20i+40j-20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641876} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6e287f-a382-5a39-b871-711f7a58afba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=-20i+40j-20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641935} {:answer/id #uuid "86d30bcb-9cf0-5243-afec-e25e11e9dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=-20i+40j-20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642040} {:answer/id #uuid "906e4b98-1fbc-596c-a470-3487068d4f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=20i-40j+20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642040} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f451ea-2c4b-5ff1-86b0-1aa6cb8b7afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4edac059-d66a-5696-903f-d640b1a9c2a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-32928" :question/problem "A line passes through the points $P(-2,2,6)$ and $Q(3,7,11).$ Find its vector equation in the form $r\\times v=c.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,2,6)$ and $Q(3,7,11).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, starting with the equation of the line using the cross product, we manipulate the equation and then substitute in our values of $p$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =-20i+40j-20k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b343c1e0-f71e-5b4f-af34-e06003a81b49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28793ddc-9623-56ad-b4a2-6ef31da4653a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-9+5t \\\\ y &=-8+3t \\\\ z &=2+6t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641895} {:answer/id #uuid "7764fc2d-0b1a-5d63-b873-cfbcf6fc729c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5t \\\\ y &=3t \\\\ z &=6t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642011} {:answer/id #uuid "e63114cd-7dde-531a-92a9-4276e121114e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-9t \\\\ y &=-8t \\\\ z &=2t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642162} {:answer/id #uuid "e73a2b36-b2c8-5678-a8b2-0e51430c910a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5-9t \\\\ y &=-3-8t \\\\ z &=-6+2t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642164} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f894e8-c8ae-51b2-af8e-866aa942b716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5-9t \\\\ y &=3-8t \\\\ z &=6+2t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642190} :answer-field/id #uuid "c36c71c2-dc3f-5415-ba7f-c5d455d31cb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d608641-69c4-5d2c-af3e-4d60668036b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72087" :question/problem "The vector equation of a line is given by \n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$\n\nFind its parametric equations." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\times v=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this general form to the given equation, we find that\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line, and\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(5,3,6)$ that lies on the line.\n\nTherefore, the given vector equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9t \\\\ -8t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-9t \\\\ 3-8t \\\\ 6+2t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand finally, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5-9t \\\\ y &=3-8t \\\\ z &=6+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "b8d1475c-c6fb-5a83-9b5b-92c75bac9ef2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642370}}] :activity/title "Writing the Vector Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641807}]}
